\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 {\listtext	\'95	}\uc0\u55357 \u57057 \u65039  Rule 2: Sudo Bruteforce Attempt - Detailed Presentation\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa319\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 \strokec2 Detection Rule: Sudo Bruteforce Attempt\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \strokec2 1. Explanation & Rationale:
\f1\b0 \uc0\u8232 This rule looks for patterns of failed 
\f2\fs26 sudo
\f1\fs24  commands followed by a success. A user occasionally mistyping their password is normal, but 
\f0\b several rapid failures immediately preceding a successful privilege escalation
\f1\b0  is a strong indicator of password guessing or brute-force attack. This detects attempts to compromise a standard user account and then escalate its privileges.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b \cf0 \strokec2 2. Rule Code (Sigma):\
title: Sudo Bruteforce Attempt\
id: a1b2c3d4-1234-5678-abcd-000000000002\
status: experimental\
description: >\
  Detects multiple failed sudo command attempts followed by a successful one.\
author: [Your Name]\
date: 2025/12/01\
logsource:\
  product: linux\
  service: sudo\
detection:\
  selection_fail:\
    - 'sudo: .* : user NOT in sudoers'\
    - 'sudo: .* : 3 incorrect password attempts'\
  selection_success:\
    - 'sudo: .* : COMMAND=.*'\
  timeframe: 2m\
  condition: 3 of selection_fail within timeframe and 1 of selection_success\
level: medium\
tags:\
  - attack.privilege_escalation\
  - attack.t1078 # Valid Accounts\
  - attack.t1110 # Brute Force\
\
3. Key Components & Purpose:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 logsource
\f1\b0\fs24 : Targets logs generated by the 
\f2\fs26 sudo
\f1\fs24  command.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 detection
\f1\b0\fs24 : Uses a more advanced correlation logic. It defines two sets of patterns (
\f2\fs26 selection_fail
\f1\fs24  and 
\f2\fs26 selection_success
\f1\fs24 ). The 
\f2\fs26 condition
\f1\fs24  requires that 
\f0\b at least 3 failure patterns occur within a 2-minute (
\f3\fs26 timeframe
\f0\fs24 ) window AND are followed by at least 1 success pattern
\f1\b0 . This reduces false alarms from simple user errors.\
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b\fs26 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 tags
\f1\b0\fs24 : Maps this to the 
\f0\b MITRE ATT&CK 
\f3\fs26 privilege_escalation
\f1\b0\fs24  tactic, showing an understanding that the attack is moving to a higher stage.\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f0\b \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 SIEM Deployment
\f1\b0 : In a SIEM, this rule would demonstrate 
\f0\b behavioral correlation
\f1\b0  across multiple log events over time, which is a core strength of modern detection systems.}